<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantion</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byzantion was an ancient Greek city on the European shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>, founded by colonists from Megara. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> refounded it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the fourth century CE, making it the new capital of the Roman world. Its original name gave rise to the modern term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantion.jpg" alt="Byzantion" width="312" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Evil berry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_Byzantion.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I's dedication of Constantinople on 11 May 330 CE, on the site of the ancient Greek colony of <b>Byzantion</b> at the strategic crossing of the Bosphorus, is the conventional founding moment of what later scholars called the Byzantine Empire, though its inhabitants called themselves Romans and recognised no…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Paeonians, took Amphipolis, Pydna and Potidaea in 357–356 BCE, crushed Onomarchus at the Battle of Crocus Field in 352 BCE, destroyed Olynthos in 348 BCE and, after failed sieges of Perinthus and <b>Byzantion</b> in 340 BCE, defeated Athens and Thebes at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Constantine I dedicated the rebuilt Greek city of <b>Byzantion</b>, refounded in 324, and renamed it Constantinopolis, Constantine's City.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
